[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: spine_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the BGP control-plane on spine_router_0_0 (FRR/bgpd daemon) is down. Mechanism: when bgpd stops on spine_router_0_0, all its eBGP sessions to adjacent devices immediately transition to Active/Down, while dataplane links remain UP. The other nodes still have their alternate BGP sessions to spine_router_0_1, so traffic continues and hosts retain reachability, but the fabric loses its intended redundancy.

Localization and evidence:
- On spine_router_0_0, “show bgp summary” returns “bgpd is not running” and systemctl shows “frr.service … Active: inactive (dead)”, confirming the BGP daemon (and FRR supervision) isn’t running.
- netstat on spine_router_0_0 does not show bgpd’s vty port (2605) listening, corroborating that bgpd is down.
- On peers, the neighbor corresponding to spine AS 65100 (spine_router_0_0) is stuck in Active while the other spine (AS 65101) is Established:
  - leaf_router_0_0: 172.16.0.4 (AS 65100) Active; 172.16.0.8 (AS 65101) Established.
  - leaf_router_0_1: 172.16.0.6 (AS 65100) Active; 172.16.0.10 (AS 65101) Established.
  - super_spine_router_0: 172.16.0.1 (AS 65100) Active; 172.16.0.3 (AS 65101) Established.
- End hosts still reach each other, proving the remaining spine path carries traffic.

Root cause: the FRR bgpd process (and frr.service) on spine_router_0_0 stopped, bringing down all BGP sessions to that spine.

Corrective fix: restart FRR on spine_router_0_0 (e.g., systemctl restart frr), verify bgpd listens and neighbors re-establish, then inspect FRR logs/core for why bgpd/watchfrr exited to prevent recurrence. === END ===